<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!-- The starting window the system draws on a launch while the editor's theme
         is light, before any of this app runs. keepStartColor (VSCodroidWebView.kt)
         names it through SplashScreen.setSplashScreenTheme, which the system keeps
         for later launches; nothing else applies it. The bar icons on it follow its
         colour by themselves.
         Theme.VSCodroid, the manifest's theme, stays dark. A launch draws it
         whenever this one is not named: under a dark editor colour, on a fresh
         install, whose editor starts on the dark default, and on the first start
         after updating from a build that never named this, whatever the editor's
         theme. A resource cannot know the editor's theme, and one following the
         device's night mode would turn those starts white for a dark editor on a
         device in light mode.
         In this directory, and not in values, because the attribute is API 31:
         an older platform has no such attribute, and a vendor's own attribute
         can carry the same id. The empty declaration in values is what gives the
         name a default.
         ⚠️ Keep the name. The system stores it as a name and looks it up on every
         launch, and one that no longer resolves falls back to the manifest's dark
         theme without an error. keepStartColor names the theme again only when the
         editor's colour changes, so after a rename an install stays dark until it
         does. -->
    <style name="Theme.VSCodroid.LightStart">
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground">@android:color/white</item>
    </style>

</resources>
